0,Benue–Congo languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benue–Congo_languages,"Benue–Congo is a major branch of the Volta-Congo languages which covers most of Sub-Saharan Africa.The neighbouring Volta–Niger branch of Nigeria and Benin is sometimes called ""West Benue–Congo"", but it does not form a united branch with Benue–Congo.",Fungwa,Niger-Congo
1,Fungwa | Ethnologue Free,https://www.ethnologue.com/language/ula/?cf=1&token=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9..Ms4rLiRsBq72QcBcACb-8L81U0aS9ltzkbpGrckTLio,Fungwa is a stable indigenous language of Nigeria. It belongs to the Niger-Congo language family. The language is used as a first language by all in the ethnic community. It is not known to be taught in schools.,Fungwa,Niger-Congo
2,Fungwa,https://lingweb.eva.mpg.de/channumerals/Fungwa.htm,"Language name and location: Fungwa, Niger state, Nigeria [Refer to Ethnologue].As a result of this, various aspects of their culture (e.g. religion, folktales and music) are being forgotten. Only about 20 of 1000 speakers of Cifungwa currently practice their culture/religion.",Fungwa,Niger-Congo
